    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Formed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> in 1825 and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a> as constitutional capital and La Paz as seat of government.</li><li>Lost wars cost it the Pacific coast to Chile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-chaco">Chaco</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>.</li><li>Its 1952 revolution and the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evo-morales">Evo Morales</a> made it a landmark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American social and indigenous politics.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Landlocked since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> still maintains a navy, which patrols <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> and the country’s rivers.</p></aside>
    </section>

